icache: handleSMC must walk the entries a fetchModeMask >= 0x80 hashes a trace into

Trace cache entries are indexed by hash(pAddr, fetchModeMask) =
(pAddr & (BxICacheEntries-1)) ^ fetchModeMask, and handleSMC() finds the
traces a store may have modified by walking, for every 128-byte page line the
store touched, the 128 entries that line's instructions hash to. That walk
assumed fetchModeMask < 0x80: XOR-ing a value below 128 into the index keeps
an entry inside its own line's 128 entries. BX_FETCH_MODE_AMX_OK is bit 7
(0x80), so as soon as XCR0 enables XTILECFG|XTILEDATA every trace lands in
the entries of the neighbouring line, the walk never reaches it, and a store
into code that already executed leaves the stale trace in place: the CPU
keeps executing the old bytes while memory holds the new ones. Any
self-modifying code under an AMX-enabled OS hits this; this crate hit it the
moment the shim set XCR0 = 0x600E7, because its step-by-step harness rewrites
the instruction at RIP between steps (x87 FXRSTOR/FNSTSW sequences read back
the state of the previously executed instruction).

Extend the walk over the lines an instruction's entry can be displaced into
(n ^ (fetchModeMask >> 7)); the per-entry traceMask test is unchanged, so
this only adds entries to look at, never flushes more than before. The hash
itself is left alone on purpose: nothing flushes the icache when XCR0 or
CR4.OSXSAVE change, so the mode bits in the index are what keeps a trace
decoded with AMX_OK clear from being executed once it is set.

Found by this crate's differential suite the day AMX was first compiled into
the vendored core; see docs/backend-differences.md §4p. Still present on
upstream master as of 2026-09-12.

The cpu.h hunk only fixes the fetchModeMask bit table in the comment above the
enum, which listed a non-existent OPMASK_OK and put EVEX_OK/AMX_OK one bit too
high (bits 7/8; the enum has them at 6/7).

The hunks below are deliberately comment-light, so that this file can be sent
upstream as-is.

